<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maliki school</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maliki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maliki school is one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools of Islamic law, named after the jurist Malik ibn Anas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. It became dominant in North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> was strongly committed to Maliki jurisprudence, and the school also shaped law in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> and Ayyubid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maliki-school.jpg" alt="Maliki school" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: chococliff · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_of_Oqba_Courtyard,_Kairouan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Aghlabid emirs were Mu'tazilites who held that the Qur'an was created, a doctrine the <b>Maliki</b> majority of Ifriqiya rejected; the scholar Sahsun was persecuted under Muhammad I for refusing it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…started around 1035–1040 CE, when Yahya ibn Ibrahim, a chieftain of the Godala (Gudala) tribe returning from pilgrimage to Mecca, met the Fez-born <b>Maliki</b> jurist Abu Imran al-Fasi in Kairouan and was eventually provided with a teacher, Abdallah ibn Yasin, to instruct his people in orthodox Maliki practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Justice followed the <b>Maliki school</b> of Sunni law, administered by qadis led by the chief qadi of Córdoba, and the sahib al-shurta kept order in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Maliki school</b> of law, introduced by scholars such as Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi, became dominant, giving the jurists of Córdoba great social influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious life followed the <b>Maliki school</b> of Sunni law, and Sufi brotherhoods and frontier mosques flourished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was overwhelmingly Sunni and <b>Maliki</b>, with Jewish communities in Tunis and other towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
